@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-reject-json-pointer-tokens-with-a-bare-unescaped-tilde
+type: decision-log
+title: "Reject JSON Pointer tokens with a bare, unescaped tilde"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6901 §3 defines a reference token's grammar as `*( unescaped / escaped )`, where `unescaped`
+is every character except `/` and `~`, and `escaped` is exactly the two-character sequence `"~0"`
+or `"~1"`. A `~` that is not immediately followed by `0` or `1` therefore has no production in the
+grammar at all — it is a syntax error, not a literal character. `decodeToken` (`src/index.ts`,
+shared by `resolvePointer` and every `applyPatch` operation via `parsePointer`) previously decoded
+tokens with two unconditional `.replace()` passes (`~1` → `/`, then `~0` → `~`) and never checked
+for this case, so pointers like `/foo~bar`, `/foo~2`, or a trailing `/foo~` were silently accepted
+and treated as containing a literal `~`, instead of being rejected.
+
+## Decision
+
+`decodeToken` now tests the raw (pre-decode) token against `/~(?![01])/` and throws before
+decoding if it matches, instead of only transforming known-good escapes. This is the single
+tokenizing entry point for every pointer-consuming code path (`resolvePointer`'s `step` reduction,
+and `opAdd`/`opRemove`/`opReplace`/`opMove`/`opCopy`/`opTest`, all of which route through
+`parsePointer`), so the fix applies uniformly to both `path` and `from` members without touching
+any operation-specific code.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A JSON Pointer containing a bare `~` (mid-token, trailing, or followed by anything other than
+  `0`/`1`) is now a thrown error everywhere a pointer is accepted — `resolvePointer` and all six
+  `applyPatch` operations — rather than being silently accepted with the `~` taken literally.
+  Any caller that was relying on the old lenient behaviour (e.g. passing an un-escaped `~` and
+  expecting it to address a key containing a literal `~`) will now see that rejected; per RFC 6901
+  such callers were already producing invalid pointers and must escape `~` as `~0`.
+  - Future additions to pointer handling (e.g. if the project ever adds URI Fragment Identifier
+    Representation support, which RFC 6901 §6 defines separately) should keep validating against
+    the same ABNF rather than re-introducing a blind-replace decoder.
