@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+id: task-003-fix-json-pointer-tilde-escape-validation-in-applypatch-resolvepointer
+type: task
+title: "Fix JSON Pointer tilde-escape validation in applyPatch/resolvePointer"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Reports that `applyPatch` accepts some patches users consider invalid, produces unexpected
+results on some edge cases, and does not always fail the way the standard says it should. Review
+how patches are checked and applied, and bring the behaviour in line with RFC 6902 (and the RFC
+6901 JSON Pointer syntax it depends on) in every case, including error cases.
+
+## What I did
+
+- Re-verified the existing implementation against: every RFC 6902 §A example, the full
+  `json-patch/json-patch-tests` community conformance suite (92 cases covering out-of-bounds
+  indices, `1e0`/leading-zero rejection, missing/`null` members, aliasing on `copy`, etc. — all
+  passing already), and a battery of additional hand-written edge cases (root operations, `move`
+  self-reference, `-0`/`0` equality, sparse validation ordering). All of that passed before any
+  change, so the defect was elsewhere.
+- Found the actual gap in `decodeToken` (`src/index.ts`), shared by every pointer-consuming
+  operation via `parsePointer`: RFC 6901 §3's ABNF only allows `~` inside a reference token as the
+  first character of the two-character escapes `~0` and `~1` — a bare `~` not followed by `0` or
+  `1` is not part of the grammar at all and must be a syntax error. The old implementation just
+  ran two blind `.replace()` passes, so a pointer like `/foo~bar`, `/foo~2`, or a trailing
+  `/foo~` was silently accepted and treated as containing a literal `~`, instead of being rejected
+  as a malformed JSON Pointer. This matches all three symptoms in the report: an objectively
+  invalid patch was accepted, its result (treating `~` literally) was not what a user reading the
+  grammar would expect, and a patch that *should* fail against a malformed pointer did not.
+  Confirmed empirically that the old code accepted `/foo~bar`, `/foo~`, `/foo~2`, `/foo~~`, both
+  via `resolvePointer` and via `applyPatch`'s `add`.
+- Fixed by validating the raw (pre-decode) token against `/~(?![01])/` in `decodeToken` and
+  throwing before decoding; this is the single tokenizing entry point used by `resolvePointer` and
+  every `op*` function (`opAdd`/`opRemove`/`opReplace` via `parsePointer`, `opMove`/`opCopy`/
+  `opTest` via `resolvePointer`/`parsePointer` on both `path` and `from`), so the fix applies
+  uniformly with one change.
+- Test-first: added three red cases to `src/index.test.ts` (bare `~` mid-token, trailing `~` with
+  nothing after it, `~` followed by a digit other than `0`/`1`), confirmed they failed against the
+  unmodified code (`node --test`: 3 failing), then implemented the fix and confirmed green (45/45).
+  Re-ran the full community conformance suite (92/92) and the RFC §A examples afterwards: no
+  regressions.
+- Type-checked `src/index.ts` in isolation against the project's strict `tsconfig.json` options,
+  using a temporary, uncommitted `typescript` install (same approach as dl-001/task-002): clean.
+  Did not add `typescript`, `@types/node`, or a typecheck script to the project.
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `src/index.ts`: `decodeToken` now validates that every `~` in a reference token is immediately
+  followed by `0` or `1` before decoding, throwing otherwise.
+- `src/index.test.ts`: added three tests for the rejected malformed-escape cases.
